{
  "requirement": "Inspection preserves the requested path scope and the selected language for\noutlining and structural search.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject `requirements.sdk.sources.provider_results` has two obligations: inspection keeps the requested path scope, and it keeps the selected language for outlining (parse) and structural search. The tests cover both.\n\n**Path scope**\n- `defaults_use_provider_primitives_and_keep_one_parse_snapshot` uses the `Memory` fixture, which lists `src/one.txt` and `other/two.txt`. It searches with prefix `src` and asserts that the provider saw `list:src`, that only `src/one.txt` was read, and that exactly 2 matches came back. This shows the scope reaches the listing and filters the results.\n- `structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope` uses the `CachedParse` fixture, which lists `src/one.rs` and `other/two.rs`. It runs structural search with prefix `src`. It asserts that the recorded scopes are `[\"src\",\"src\",\"src\"]`, that there is exactly one match, and that the match path is `src/one.rs`. The out-of-scope file is therefore excluded.\n- `gitignore_is_shared_by_all_operations_with_or_without_a_git_repository` in `tests/source_defaults.rs` checks real scoping. It searches with `./src/../src` and gets only `src/nested.txt`. Searching an ignored path or `skip` returns nothing. A prefix of `..` is an error.\n- `scoped_inspection_skips_unrelated_directories_and_reports_relevant_ignore_errors` asserts that listing with scope `src` returns only `src/one.txt` and ignores the broken sibling directory.\n\n**Selected language**\n- `list_read_only_provider_supports_selected_language_parse_and_structural_search` parses with the `Primitives` provider. The provider's explicit language `rust` takes priority over a `.py` extension, and with no explicit language the `.rs` extension selects Rust. In both cases it asserts the outline resolves `fn[provided]`.\n- The same test runs structural search with language `rust` and asserts the match, selector and path. With language `python` it asserts a complete, empty result. With `not-a-language` it asserts an error. A malformed rule is also an error.\n- `structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope` asserts that the rust query yields the match from the overridden parse.\n\nThe subtopics `parse_snapshot` and `search_completeness` have their own requirements, and their tests are marked here too. I did not reassess them.\n\nI found no plausible violation of the scope or language preservation text that these tests would accept.",
  "references": "fb25fa326e584dedcc46b4cb5ffb943c498c41361dc844a88aafec157fd70a3f",
